NAME
       rpm - query, install and check RPM packages

SYNOPSIS
       rpm -q [-a | -f FILE | -p PACKAGE-FILE] [-i] [-l] [-c] [-d] [-R]
              [--scripts] [--changelog] [NAME...]
       rpm {-i | -U | -F} [-v] [-h] [--nodeps] [--test] PACKAGE-FILE...
       rpm -e [--nodeps] NAME...
       rpm -V NAME...
       rpm -K PACKAGE-FILE...
       rpm --import KEYFILE

DESCRIPTION
       rpm is the low-level package tool of RHEL. It keeps the database of
       installed packages in /var/lib/rpm and can install a package file,
       but it does not download anything and does not resolve dependencies.
       For installing and updating, use dnf, which uses rpm underneath; rpm
       is mostly used to ask questions.

   Query (-q)
       rpm -q NAME prints the full name (name-version-release.arch) of an
       installed package, or "package NAME is not installed". What to show
       and which packages to ask about can be combined:

       -a          every installed package
       -f FILE     the package that owns FILE
       -p FILE     a package file (.rpm) instead of an installed package
       -i          information: version, size, summary, description
       -l          the files in the package
       -c          only its configuration files
       -d          only its documentation files
       -R          what it requires
       --scripts   the scripts it runs at install and removal
       --changelog the change history

       --whatprovides WHAT and --whatrequires WHAT find the installed
       packages that provide or need WHAT.

OPTIONS
       -i, --install FILE
              Install a package file. Fails if dependencies are missing.

       -U, --upgrade FILE
              Install or upgrade.

       -F, --freshen FILE
              Upgrade only if an older version is installed.

       -v, -h Verbose output and a progress bar of # signs (-ivh, -Uvh).

       --nodeps
              Skip the dependency checks (rarely a good idea).

       --test Check only, install nothing.

       -e, --erase NAME
              Remove a package.

       -V, --verify NAME
              Compare the installed files with the package: each changed file
              gets a line of letters (S size, 5 checksum, M mode, T time...).

       -K, --checksig FILE
              Check a package file's digests and signature.

       --import KEYFILE
              Import a GPG public key, so that packages signed with it are
              trusted. Red Hat's key is in /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/.

EXAMPLES
       Is httpd installed, and which version?

           rpm -q httpd

       Which package owns a file, and what else is in it:

           rpm -qf /etc/chrony.conf
           rpm -ql chrony

       Configuration files and documentation of a package:

           rpm -qc openssh-server
           rpm -qd openssh-server

       Information about a package file before installing it, then install
       it with dnf (which brings the dependencies):

           rpm -qip /mnt/BaseOS/Packages/tree-*.rpm
           dnf install /mnt/BaseOS/Packages/tree-*.rpm

       Trust Red Hat's signing key:

           rpm --import /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release

       Count the installed packages:

           rpm -qa | wc -l

SEE ALSO
       dnf(8), rpmkeys(8), yum(8)
